1.  **Motor Control with VNH3SP30 Driver**: The VNH3SP30 is a full H-bridge motor driver that can handle currents up to 30A. You can use it to control the speed of your cart's motor. The VNH5019A-E chips are newer and almost identical to the VNH3SP30 chips, but they work with 3.3V and 5V logic, while the older VNH3SP30 chips require 5V logic [[1](https://github.com/bmellink/VNH3SP30)].
2.  **PWM Speed Regulation**: Pulse-width modulation (PWM) is a technique used to control the speed of DC motors. You can use the Arduino's PWM capabilities to regulate the speed of your cart's motor. The Arduino Diecimila and Duemilanove models use the ATmega168 or ATmega328, which can implement PWM [[3](https://www.arduino.cc/en/Tutorial/SecretsOfArduinoPWM)].
3.  **Ultrasonic Sensor Integration**: You'll need to integrate 3 ultrasonic sensors to detect obstacles and follow a human. You can use the PWMMotorControl library, which contains examples for controlling brushed DC motors using PWM and standard full bridge IC's like the L298 or SparkFun Motor Driver - Dual TB6612FNG [[2](https://github.com/ArminJo/PWMMotorControl)].
